Singleton Design Pattern:
-------------------------
	Singleton pattern ensures only one instance of the class exists in the entire application.
	
when to use:
	->exactly one object is needed.
	->Shared resources must be controlled.
	->state must be consistent accross the application.
	
Realtime usecases:
------------------
->database connection manager
->logger
->configuration manager
->Cache Manager
->Thread pool manager


Database Connection Manager Example:
-----------------------------------

Problem without Singleton pattern is Multple database object created and leads to resource leak / performance issues.

Solution using Singleton Pattern:

Eager Loading:Simple and Thread Safe

DatabaseConnection.java:

package com.design;

public class DatabaseConnection {
	private static final DatabaseConnection dbConnectonInstance = new DatabaseConnection();

	// private construction to prevent external instantiation
	private DatabaseConnection() {

	}

	public static DatabaseConnection getConnection() {
		return dbConnectonInstance;
	}

	public void connect() {
		System.out.println("Conntected to db:");
	}
}

Main class:

package com.design;

public class UseDbConnection {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		// DatabaseConnection obj=new DatabaseConnection();//The constructor
		// DatabaseConnection() is not visible
		DatabaseConnection db1 = DatabaseConnection.getConnection();
		DatabaseConnection db2 = DatabaseConnection.getConnection();
		db1.connect();
		System.out.println(db1 == db2);
	}
}



